WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:04.295
The US has woken up to a big risk
when it comes to energy.

00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:06.881
15 years ago, China could produce six times

00:00:06.881 --> 00:00:09.009
as many solar panels as the US

00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:11.011
Look at what's happened since then.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:13.847
It now can make 70 times as many.

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:17.350
That massive production capacity
means China alone is able

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:21.354
to install more than half of the world's
new solar panels this year.

00:00:21.438 --> 00:00:22.063
What's more,

00:00:22.063 --> 00:00:26.067
it means that the relatively little solar
capacity installed in the US

00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:29.529
is made abroad, often
with components from China.

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:32.407
Beijing’s dominance in this space
is a big deal

00:00:32.407 --> 00:00:33.992
because solar energy will be

00:00:33.992 --> 00:00:36.536
the dominant source of renewable energy

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:38.038
by the end of the decade.

00:00:38.121 --> 00:00:41.666
And the world is dependent on China
for the technology.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.627
It's a vulnerability
the US wants to fix.

00:00:44.627 --> 00:00:49.591
I've long said, when I think climate,
not a joke, I think jobs.

00:00:49.674 --> 00:00:53.970
The problem is
that while America was investing

00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:56.806
sporadically in solar manufacturing,

00:00:56.806 --> 00:00:59.392
China was going all in.

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:00.935
They did everything.

00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:02.645
Solar manufacturing
is a horrible business,

00:01:02.645 --> 00:01:05.899
and nearly everyone who's gone into
it has failed.

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:09.778
The US is making a multibillion dollar
bet to get back into that

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:14.199
horrible business, as the transition
to renewable energy gathers pace.

00:01:14.282 --> 00:01:17.452
It's going to be a big ask.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.043
The impact of climate change is already very real. 

00:01:25.043 --> 00:01:28.546
The summer of 2023 was the hottest on record,

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:31.633
exceeding the blistering temperatures of just a year earlier.

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:35.053
It's the backdrop for the United Nations
annual climate talks.

00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:39.682
COP 28 wants to see a tripling
of renewables by the end of the decade.

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:43.144
Solar is likely to be
the biggest part of that, with three times

00:01:43.144 --> 00:01:47.398
as much capacity worldwide
as onshore and offshore wind by 2030.

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:50.401
Unlike other types of renewable

00:01:50.401 --> 00:01:53.863
and emission-free power,
solar can be deployed very quickly.

00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:57.075
It can be scaled up rapidly
and it can reduce emissions

00:01:57.325 --> 00:01:59.911
in a very short timescale,
which is what we need.

00:01:59.911 --> 00:02:01.788
Before we get into how the US

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:05.375
lost so much ground and President
Biden's efforts to regain some of it,

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:08.628
we need to understand
what a solar panel consists of.

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:11.798
Most of the weight
is actually just aluminum and glass.

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:16.177
But the key material at the heart of it
is silicon, because photovoltaic panels

00:02:16.177 --> 00:02:20.265
are essentially semiconductors, similar
to the chips that run your smartphone.

00:02:20.348 --> 00:02:24.519
They require a particular kind of silicon
with exceptionally high purity.

00:02:24.602 --> 00:02:27.647
Polysilicon is a raw material
to make solar modules.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:28.815
You get it from sand,

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:31.234
but you need to refine it
using a lot of energy

00:02:31.234 --> 00:02:34.612
and a lot of complicated chemical
and physical processes.

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:37.615
So it's very energy intensive to make

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:40.618
and it's very CapEx
intensive to build a factory to make it.

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:42.370
While the US used to have its own 

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.497
polysilicon manufacturing,

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:48.376
China now supplies 89% of the world's
solar grade polysilicon.

00:02:48.376 --> 00:02:50.587
That wasn’t always the case

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:55.758
Japan, Germany and the US all used to be major solar panel manufacturers.

00:02:55.758 --> 00:02:59.929
20 years ago, not only did China make very few solar panels,

00:02:59.929 --> 00:03:02.807
it had to import most of the polysilicon needed to make them.

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:06.436
Then Beijing seemed to recognize something.

00:03:06.436 --> 00:03:09.189
China was able to identify solar 

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:11.733
as an area where it could get in at the ground floor.

00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:15.778
It was an opportunity to really take over
this industry, to dominate this industry.

00:03:15.778 --> 00:03:20.033
From 2004, Chinese companies
responded to international demand

00:03:20.116 --> 00:03:23.203
by setting up bigger factories
for solar than ever before.

00:03:23.286 --> 00:03:27.916
With support from government, but
also just competing to supply a market.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:30.501
Chinese companies continued to innovate

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:31.836
in terms of pushing down costs

00:03:31.836 --> 00:03:35.131
and building up unprecedented scale
in manufacturing.

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:38.509
Those lower costs
rapidly undercut the existing players,

00:03:38.509 --> 00:03:43.932
particularly after 2008, when global capacity started to outstrip demand. 

00:03:43.932 --> 00:03:46.434
That sent prices plummeting.

00:03:46.434 --> 00:03:49.103
The first big players in solar
are mostly gone now

00:03:49.103 --> 00:03:51.648
because solar is a viciously
competitive business. 

00:03:51.648 --> 00:03:54.400
Take for example, the German company SolarWorld. 

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.363
In 2008, it was expanding in the US

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:02.909
Go solar with Solar World, America’s biggest solar panel manufacturer

00:04:02.992 --> 00:04:05.161
But frankly, it just couldn't compete.

00:04:05.161 --> 00:04:08.122
It's one of the many solar
manufacturing companies

00:04:08.122 --> 00:04:09.791
that just couldn't make it in the US.

00:04:09.791 --> 00:04:12.377
China's massive bet was starting to pay off.

00:04:12.543 --> 00:04:13.962
They pulled out all the stops, 

00:04:13.962 --> 00:04:16.756
both on the supply side and on the demand side.

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:21.094
They offered cheap financing and cheap
land to solar manufacturing facilities,

00:04:21.177 --> 00:04:24.430
and they used incentives and subsidies

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:25.807
to encourage demand.

00:04:25.807 --> 00:04:27.976
That's the other side of this coin.

00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:32.313
Beijing was also ensuring that people
in China would buy the solar gear.

00:04:32.397 --> 00:04:37.402
And critically, it didn't need to import
oil or other fossil fuels for this power.

00:04:37.402 --> 00:04:40.822
In theory at least, given that solar panels are made using coal power.

00:04:40.822 --> 00:04:44.242
All it needed was sun, sand
and a ton of money.

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:46.327
That was also good for the world.

00:04:46.327 --> 00:04:48.496
It made solar panels way cheaper,

00:04:48.496 --> 00:04:51.040
way more quickly than anyone had anticipated.

00:04:51.124 --> 00:04:54.127
China is the reason why
solar is not a cottage industry anymore.

00:04:54.252 --> 00:04:59.340
We're expecting solar new build
this year to be about 392 gigawatts,

00:04:59.424 --> 00:05:00.466
which is a lot.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:04.470
It's the equivalent in generation terms
of about one nuclear reactor

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.805
every ten days.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:09.100
More than half of those solar panels
will be installed in China.

00:05:09.183 --> 00:05:11.477
It's one hell of a boom, and US

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:13.438
manufacturers are missing out on it.

00:05:13.438 --> 00:05:16.983
Solar has just really exploded
around the world and the US

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:17.942
and other countries,

00:05:17.942 --> 00:05:22.196
frankly, kind of discounted how quickly
it would become part of the energy mix.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:24.907
Even so,
there are plenty who say that the US

00:05:24.907 --> 00:05:26.826
doesn't need to make its own panels.

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:28.703
China has made it cheap.

00:05:28.703 --> 00:05:31.331
And selling cheap
things is generally a terrible business.

00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:32.915
There's an argument that the US

00:05:32.915 --> 00:05:35.043
should just let China take on that burden,

00:05:35.043 --> 00:05:38.504
as it does with all sorts
of other electronics manufacturing.

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:41.632
Reliance on any foreign
supplier is challenging.

00:05:41.883 --> 00:05:43.885
Geopolitical concerns can get in the way.

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:46.971
And in the solar supply chain,
that's not just the solar panels, but

00:05:46.971 --> 00:05:50.516
also the inputs along the way
to making them.

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:54.479
It's the polysilicon, it's the wafers.
If any company or country

00:05:54.479 --> 00:05:57.815
is the sole supplier
of some of those inputs, they can squeeze

00:05:57.815 --> 00:05:59.317
they can squeeze on prices

00:05:59.317 --> 00:06:02.820
and they can also squeeze
on supply at any given time.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:08.201
China has a track record here. Back in 2010, a territorial dispute with Japan

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:11.037
saw Beijing restrict supply of rare-earth metals

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:14.374
used in laptop hard drives and car batteries.

00:06:14.457 --> 00:06:17.377
If the US's goal is to reduce that kind of risk,

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:18.628
Here’s what it needs to do:

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:22.298
Boost demand,
make imports harder, add support

00:06:22.298 --> 00:06:26.469
for domestic manufacturing
and make it easier to build the projects.

00:06:26.511 --> 00:06:30.056
The core of these efforts
is 2022’s Inflation Reduction Act.

00:06:30.181 --> 00:06:32.975
The Inflation Reduction Act is very,
very generous.

00:06:32.975 --> 00:06:35.603
Every wafer, every cell, every module 

00:06:35.603 --> 00:06:39.148
that is made in the US
factory gets a tax credit.

00:06:39.148 --> 00:06:41.692
There's also tons of support
in the Inflation Reduction Act

00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:43.736
on the demand side of the equation.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:46.531
So there's direct money, $7 billion of it,

00:06:46.531 --> 00:06:50.701
designed to go to rooftop and community solar projects.

00:06:50.701 --> 00:06:54.455
 Expect solar to rise
on the agenda after COP 28 in Dubai.

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:56.374
China got a head start on this tech,

00:06:56.374 --> 00:06:58.000
but that doesn't mean the US

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:59.919
has completely missed out.

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:01.462
I think the best case for the US

00:07:01.462 --> 00:07:04.465
is that it continues to support
a domestic manufacturing industry

00:07:04.674 --> 00:07:06.634
and have some control
of its supply chains,

00:07:06.634 --> 00:07:09.512
even if it pays a little bit
more than most countries do for solar.

00:07:09.512 --> 00:07:12.265
The US needs to get all in,

00:07:12.265 --> 00:07:15.852
and it needs to do everything right,
and it needs to do it for a long time.

00:07:15.852 --> 00:07:17.520
It has to have staying power.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.273
The lesson from the last decade
is that the US

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:21.858
has to be committed and it has to 

00:07:21.858 --> 00:07:24.235
come to this as committed as China.

